"Reconstruction of coefficients in scalar second-order elliptic equations from knowledge of their solutions (https://arxiv.org/abs/1111.5051)\nciteturn1000academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.9 years ago; Authors: Guillaume Bal, Gunther Uhlmann ... This paper concerns the reconstruction of possibly complex-valued coefficients in a second-order scalar elliptic equation posed on a bounded domain from knowledge of several solutions of that equation.\nTitle: Reconstruction of coefficients in scalar second-order elliptic equations from knowledge of their solutions\nAuthors: Guillaume Bal, Gunther Uhlmann\nDate: Mon Nov 21 22:37:04 2011\n\nThis paper concerns the reconstruction of possibly complex-valued coefficients in a second-order scalar elliptic equation posed on a bounded domain from knowledge of several solutions of that equation. We show that for a sufficiently large number of solutions and for an open set of corresponding boundary conditions, all coefficients can be uniquely and stably reconstructed up to a well characterized gauge transformation. We also show that in some specific situations, a minimum number of such available solutions equal to $I_n=\\frac12n(n+3)$ is sufficient to uniquely and globally reconstruct the unknown coefficients. This theory finds applications in several coupled-physics medical imaging modalities including photo-acoustic tomography, transient elastography, and magnetic resonance elastography.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReconstruction of coeﬃcients in scalar second-order (https://www.stat.uchicago.edu/~guillaumebal/PAPERS/Hybrid-Meas-u-12.pdf)\nciteturn1000search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Reconstruction of coeﬃcients in scalar second-order ... Guillaume Bal ∗Gunther Uhlmann † ... This paper concerns the reconstruction of possibly complex-valued coeﬃcients\nReconstruction of coeﬃcients in scalar second-order\nelliptic equations from knowledge of their solutions\nGuillaume Bal ∗\nGunther Uhlmann †\nApril 10, 2013\nAbstract\nThis paper concerns the reconstruction of possibly complex-valued coeﬃcients\nin a second-order scalar elliptic equation posed on a bounded domain from knowl-\nedge of several solutions of that equation. We show that for a suﬃciently large\nnumber of solutions and for an open set of corresponding boundary conditions, all\ncoeﬃcients can be uniquely and stably reconstructed up to a well characterized\ngauge transformation. We also show that in some speciﬁc situations, a minimum\nnumber of such available solutions equal to In = 1\n2n(n+3) is suﬃcient to uniquely\nand globally reconstruct the unknown coeﬃcients. This theory ﬁnds applications\nin several coupled-physics medical imaging modalities including photo-acoustic\ntomography, transient elastography, and magnetic resonance elastography.\n1\nIntroduction\nWe consider the general second-order elliptic equation:\n∇· a∇u + b · ∇u + cu = 0\nin X,\nu = f\non ∂X,\n(1)\nwith complex-valued coeﬃcients such that a is a symmetric, complex-valued tensor\nverifying the ellipticity condition for α0 > 0:\nα0|ξ|2 ≤ξ · ℜaξ ≤α−1\n0 |ξ|2,\n(2)\nand with (b, c) such that the above equation admits a unique solution in H1(X) for\nf ∈H\n1\n2(∂X). Here X is an open bounded domain in Rn with smooth boundary ∂X.\nWe assume throughout this paper that b, c and ∇·a are of class C0,α( ¯X) for some α > 0\nand that a is of class C0,1( ¯X). Elliptic regularity results [20, Theorem 6.3.7] then ensure\nthat the solution u is a strong solution of class C2,α( ¯X) when the boundary condition\nis of class C2,α(∂X).\n∗Department of Applied Physics and Applied Mathematics, Columbia University, New York NY,\n10027; gb2030@columbia.edu\n†Department of Mathematics, University of Washington, Seattle, WA, 98195 and University of\nCalifornia, Irvine, CA, 92697; gunther@math.washington.edu\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCommunications on Pure